<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Umayyad Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/705">705</a>–<a href="/years/715">715</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Great Mosque of Damascus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Umayyad Mosque, or Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, was built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a> between 705 and 715 on the site of a Christian church of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>, which had replaced a Roman temple of Jupiter. Its vast courtyard, prayer hall and mosaics of landscapes and buildings, made with Byzantine craftsmen, set a model for later congregational mosques, and it still holds a shrine said to contain the head of John the Baptist.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque.jpg" alt="Umayyad Mosque" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umayyad_Mosque,_Damascus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The site has been a place of worship since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, when the Arameans of Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> built a temple to Hadad-Ramman, god of thunderstorms and rain. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> it was dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, and after Roman conquest of Damascus in 64 BCE it became a temple of Jupiter, enlarged probably from the reign of Augustus. The Roman temenos walls still form the outer walls of the mosque.</p><p>Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> turned the temple into a cathedral. After the Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 634 a prayer room, a musalla, was set up in part of the building, and the Christians kept the rest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">Al-Walid I</a>, the sixth Umayyad caliph, took over the cathedral, returning other properties in the city to the Christians as compensation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The mosque was built over nine years by thousands of labourers and artisans from the Islamic and Byzantine worlds, paid for from the war booty of Umayyad conquests and taxes on the Arab troops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. Al-Walid supervised the work and told the people of Damascus that he wished to add a fifth distinction to their climate, water, fruits and baths: this mosque. The plan has a large courtyard on the north, measuring with the whole complex 97 by 156 metres, and a prayer hall along the south wall with three parallel aisles crossed by a central nave leading to the mihrab.</p><p>The architect re-used the columns and arcades of the church. Gold mosaics of vegetal motifs covered some 4,000 square metres and are likely the largest in the world. Later rulers added to it. The Abbasid governor built the Dome of the Treasury in 789–90, and a minaret, the Minaret of the Bride, is credited to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> ordered extensive restoration of its marble, mosaics and gilding in 1270, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> added the Minaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qaitbay">Qaitbay</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Medieval Muslim writers regarded the mosque as a wonder of the world. It is considered the oldest mosque still in use in its original form, and one of the few early mosques to keep the general structure of the early 8th century. Its plan was the model for congregational mosques, among them al-Azhar and the Baybars Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, the Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Cordoba</a> and the Grand Mosques of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>.</p><p>Christians and Muslims alike hold that the head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> is buried there, a tradition from the 6th century, and two shrines commemorate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>. One <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> places the descent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> at a white minaret in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, which tradition identifies with the Minaret of Isa; most Muslim theologians read this symbolically.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Umayyad Mosque” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Mosque of Damascus</b> was built in the reign of his son al-Walid I (r. 705–715).</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The story goes back to the 10th-century historian al-Razi and echoes accounts of the <b>Umayyad Mosque</b> in Damascus.</blockquote><a href="/work/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
